    {
      "id": "https://www.ziarulnational.md/de-la-agricultura-la-aigricultura-tehnologia-transforma-fermele/",
      "url": "https://www.ziarulnational.md/de-la-agricultura-la-aigricultura-tehnologia-transforma-fermele/",
      "title": "Farmers adopt AI tools as sector seeks lasting digital business models",
      "content_html": "Farmers in Northern Ireland are using AI-powered cameras to detect changes in cattle health, robotic systems to automate milking and digital apps to manage records. Lantmännen says AI can speed up crop breeding and improve the use of fertiliser, crop-protection products and water. The applications range from cameras that analyse daily videos of cows to robotic milking and digital farm records. Technology company Cgrain, majority-owned by Lantmännen, uses image analysis to assess grain quality.\n\nA Nairobi meeting scheduled for Oct. 27 will examine financing, partnerships and service integration to sustain digital agriculture after development funding ends. An investment study tracked $4.39 billion in agtech funding across 17 emerging markets from 2023 through the third quarter of 2025, finding a recovery after a post-2022 correction but uneven performance. Lantmännen’s Per Arfvidsson said wider AI benefits depend on reliable data and practical value for farms, and argued that farmers must retain control of their data and strategic decisions.",
